Did you define the sequence first?>
$$c_k = k + \cfrac{1}{2k + \cfrac{1}{2k + \cfrac{1}{2k + \dotsb}}}$$
$$\implies c_k = k + \frac{1}{2k + \frac{1}{c_k}}$$
$$\implies c_k = k + \frac{1}{\frac{2kc_k + 1}{c_k}}$$
$$\implies c_k^2 + kc_k = kc_k + \frac{k}{c_k} + 1$$
$$\implies c_k^2 = \frac{k+1}{c_k} + 1$$
Multiplying both MPC with $c_k$ on both sides,
$$c_k^3 = (k+1) + c_k$$
and hence, $(c_k^2)^2 = ((k+1) + c_k)(c_k)$
\begin{align}
(c_1^2)^2 & = (2 + c_1) c_1\\
(c_2^2)^2 & = (3 + c_2) c_2\\
(c_3^2)^2 & = (4 + c_3) c_3\\
&~~~~\vdots\\
(c_{11}^2)^2 & = (12 + c_{11} )c_{11} 
\end{align}
Summing the above $10$ equations,
\begin{align}
\sum_{k = 1}^{11} (c_k^2)^2 & = (2 + \dots + 12) + (c_1 + \dots + c_{11}) \\
& = \frac{2+12+1}{2}.11 + C_n + C_{n-1} \\
& = \frac{16}{2}.11 + C_n + C_{n-1} 
\boldsymbol{\implies c_1 = 1+ \frac{1}{2 \cdot 1 + \frac{1}{^{\ddots}}}
}\end{align}
where, $C_n$ is the $n^\text{th}$ convergent of the continued fraction. Here, I've used the $11^\text{th}$ convergent as an approximation of $c_1$. With this approximation, your answer becomes
$$ \boxed{ 144.45}{^*} $$
：<end answer>

